function LearnAboutPeriods({ onBack, onStartTracking }) {
  return (
    <div style={{ maxWidth: 480, margin: "0 auto" }}>
      <TopBar title="Know about Periods 🩷" onBack={onBack} />
      <div style={{ padding: "4px 20px 6px", fontSize: 13.5, color: COLORS.plum500, lineHeight: 1.6 }}>
        A simple, friendly guide for everyone — girls, boys, and anyone curious. Understanding periods helps build empathy and kindness. 💛
      </div>

      <div style={{ padding: "14px 20px 0", display: "flex", flexDirection: "column", gap: 14 }}>
        <InfoSection emoji="🩸" title="What Is a Period?">
          A period (or menstruation) is a completely natural part of the monthly cycle in a girl's or woman's body.
          Roughly once a month, the body sheds the lining of the uterus, which leaves the body as blood over a few
          days — usually 3 to 7 days. It's a normal, healthy sign the body is working properly. Most people start
          getting periods sometime between ages 9 and 15, and periods continue until menopause, usually in a
          person's late 40s or 50s.
        </InfoSection>

        <InfoSection emoji="🔄" title="Why Does It Happen?">
          Every month, the body prepares for a possible pregnancy by thickening the lining of the uterus. If
          pregnancy doesn't happen, the body doesn't need that lining, so it sheds it — that's the period. This
          cycle repeats roughly every 21–35 days, though the exact timing is different for everyone.
        </InfoSection>

        <InfoSection emoji="🤔" title="Myths vs Facts">
          <MythFact
            myth="Periods are dirty or impure."
            fact="Periods are a normal biological process. There's nothing shameful or unclean about it."
          />
          <MythFact
            myth="You can't do normal activities during your period."
            fact="Most people can go to school, play sports, swim, and carry on with everyday life during their period."
          />
          <MythFact
            myth="Only girls and women need to know about periods."
            fact="Everyone benefits from understanding periods — it builds empathy and more supportive communities for all genders."
          />
        </InfoSection>

        <InfoSection emoji="🧻" title="Understanding Pads & Napkins">
          Sanitary pads (also called napkins) are worn inside underwear to absorb period blood. There are also
          other options like tampons and menstrual cups — people choose whatever feels most comfortable for them.
          As a general rule of thumb, pads are usually changed every 4–6 hours, or sooner if needed, to stay clean
          and comfortable. Used pads should be wrapped and placed in a bin rather than flushed, which keeps things
          hygienic for everyone.
        </InfoSection>

        <InfoSection emoji="💛" title="It's Okay to Feel Different">
          Periods can come with cramps, tiredness, mood changes, or bloating — this is normal and caused by
          hormonal shifts, not weakness or overreacting. Rest, a warm compress, staying hydrated, and a little
          patience with yourself (or with someone going through it) can make a real difference.
        </InfoSection>

        <InfoSection emoji="🤝" title="How Everyone Can Help">
          Boys and men can be great allies simply by treating periods as a normal topic, not something awkward or
          taboo. Small things matter — offering a pad if someone needs one, not joking about periods, and being
          understanding if someone isn't feeling their best. Open, respectful conversations help remove stigma for
          everyone, regardless of gender.
        </InfoSection>

        <InfoSection emoji="💬" title="A Quick Note">
          This page is meant for general awareness and isn't a substitute for advice from a parent, teacher, or
          doctor. If you have questions about your own body or symptoms that worry you, talking to a trusted adult
          or healthcare provider is always a good idea.
        </InfoSection>

        <Card style={{ textAlign: "center", background: COLORS.cardAlt, border: "none" }}>
          <div style={{ fontFamily: "'Fraunces', serif", fontWeight: 600, fontSize: 16, color: COLORS.plum900, marginBottom: 6 }}>
            Want to track your own cycle?
          </div>
          <div style={{ fontSize: 13, color: COLORS.plum600, marginBottom: 14 }}>
            You can set up personalized tracking and predictions any time.
          </div>
          <PrimaryButton onClick={onStartTracking}>Track My Period <Droplet size={16} /></PrimaryButton>
        </Card>
      </div>
    </div>
  );
}

